WEBVTT

00:00.450 --> 00:04.277
This is Part 7: safety, tools, and job day.

00:04.227 --> 00:07.572
You keep yourself, and everyone on site, safe.

00:08.522 --> 00:13.460
Every job needs a Field Hazard Assessment, filled in and signed before work starts.

00:13.410 --> 00:15.100
No exceptions.

00:15.100 --> 00:17.507
The whole crew reads it and signs.

00:17.507 --> 00:23.572
Working up high, over six feet: a rated ladder, three-point contact, and a partner at the base.

00:23.572 --> 00:28.236
Wet or icy ground: wait for dry, or use non-slip boots and caution tape.

00:28.236 --> 00:31.442
Power lines within ten feet: move the ladder.

00:31.442 --> 00:35.215
Bumpy or soft ground: ladder levelers or solid boards.

00:35.215 --> 00:38.965
Cars or people walking by: cones out, hi-vis on.

00:38.965 --> 00:43.248
High wind, lightning or deep cold: put it off if it's not safe.

00:43.248 --> 00:47.102
Sharp scraps: cut-proof gloves, and bag them right away.

00:48.058 --> 00:49.859
You must have these first.

00:49.809 --> 00:54.381
WHMIS, for the safe use of chemicals like sealants and solvents.

00:54.381 --> 00:56.036
It must be current.

00:56.036 --> 01:00.457
Working at Heights: ladders, three-point contact, and fall safety.

01:00.458 --> 01:07.136
And electrical safety: know a hundred twenty volt dangers, GFCI plugs, and safe work near live power.

01:07.136 --> 01:09.427
Keep copies of your certificates.

01:10.386 --> 01:11.667
Ladders.

01:11.617 --> 01:15.887
Use a CSA or ANSI ladder rated for you plus your tools.

01:15.887 --> 01:19.545
Set it at four to one: one foot out for every four feet up.

01:20.498 --> 01:27.068
On pavement, on bumpy ground, or in wind, a partner holds the base, with both hands on the side rails.

01:27.970 --> 01:32.943
Keep three points of contact at all times, and move one hand or one foot at a time.

01:32.893 --> 01:36.654
Tools go in your tool belt, never in your hands while you climb.

01:36.654 --> 01:39.201
Keep your belt buckle between the rails.

01:39.201 --> 01:41.064
Don't reach too far.

01:41.064 --> 01:44.027
And stay ten feet away from power lines.

01:44.970 --> 01:49.017
On a roof, use a harness, a lanyard, and a roof anchor.

01:48.967 --> 01:52.265
No ladder in high wind, rain or lightning.

01:52.265 --> 01:55.714
And with gusts over sixty kilometres an hour, no ladder.

01:56.666 --> 01:58.063
Electrical.

01:58.013 --> 02:00.420
The lights run at twenty-four volts.

02:00.420 --> 02:03.660
That's low, and not dangerous in normal use.

02:03.660 --> 02:07.931
But the box plugs into a hundred twenty volt wall power, and that can hurt you.

02:07.931 --> 02:10.825
Always use a GFCI outdoor plug.

02:10.825 --> 02:13.753
Never touch the box or plug with wet hands.

02:13.753 --> 02:16.785
Never change the cord, or get around a breaker.

02:16.785 --> 02:19.031
GFCI keeps tripping?

02:19.031 --> 02:21.114
Stop and find out why.

02:21.114 --> 02:22.792
Don't just reset it.

02:22.792 --> 02:26.322
And unplug from the wall before you change anything in the box.

02:27.274 --> 02:28.855
The job site.

02:28.805 --> 02:33.516
Look first: ice, wet spots, soft ground, and things overhead.

02:33.516 --> 02:36.028
Put cones under where you work up high.

02:36.028 --> 02:39.176
Wear a hi-vis vest near roads and driveways.

02:39.176 --> 02:41.132
Keep it tidy all day.

02:41.132 --> 02:45.241
Coil cables, bag garbage, and put sharp tools in a pouch.

02:45.241 --> 02:47.613
Pick up track offcuts right away.

02:47.613 --> 02:49.465
Cut edges cut deep.

02:50.418 --> 02:52.000
Safety gear.

02:51.950 --> 02:55.780
Safety glasses for every cut, and any time bits can fly.

02:55.780 --> 02:58.593
Cut-proof gloves when you handle or cut track.

02:58.593 --> 03:01.475
CSA work boots for all site work.

03:01.475 --> 03:03.500
Steel toe is best.

03:03.500 --> 03:06.371
A hi-vis vest near a road or driveway.

03:06.371 --> 03:09.044
A hard hat when someone works above you.

03:09.044 --> 03:12.343
And a full first aid kit, easy to reach in the truck.

03:12.343 --> 03:15.908
Knee pads are a good idea for long kneeling on concrete.

03:16.874 --> 03:23.108
Tools you must have: a drill or impact, eighteen volts or more, with bits and a charged spare battery.

03:23.058 --> 03:25.396
Left and right cut metal snips.

03:25.396 --> 03:29.933
A CSA or ANSI extension ladder, the right size for the roof.

03:29.933 --> 03:32.051
Two tape measures per crew.

03:32.051 --> 03:40.338
A level or speed square, a utility knife, a wire stripper and crimper, a chalk line and pencil, and a rubber mallet to seat lights without cracking them.

03:40.338 --> 03:44.042
A twelve gauge outdoor extension cord for testing on the ground.

03:44.042 --> 03:46.530
A clamp meter, and a multimeter.

03:47.482 --> 03:49.966
On job day, you're Horizon.

03:49.916 --> 03:51.490
Be on time.

03:51.490 --> 03:52.960
Running late?

03:52.960 --> 03:55.634
Text or call the client thirty minutes ahead.

03:55.634 --> 03:58.169
Wear clean Horizon work clothes.

03:58.169 --> 04:01.745
Say hi, and tell the client your name and your crew's names.

04:01.745 --> 04:06.525
No smoking, vaping, loud music, or personal calls where the client can see.

04:06.525 --> 04:09.569
No damage to plants, siding or gutters.

04:09.569 --> 04:13.157
Find something odd, like hidden wire or rotten fascia?

04:13.157 --> 04:17.972
Stop, tell the client, and take a dated photo of old damage before you start.

04:17.972 --> 04:21.826
And no loud tools before eight a.m. or after six p.m.

04:22.794 --> 04:24.387
Dress for the weather.

04:24.337 --> 04:31.212
Over twenty-five degrees: sunscreen, light long sleeves, a hat, extra water, and a shade break every hour.

04:31.212 --> 04:35.552
Under five degrees: warm boots, layers, and warm gloves.

04:35.552 --> 04:38.724
Cold fingers slow you down and cause accidents.

04:38.724 --> 04:42.948
Rain: a rain jacket and boots, and no ladder in heavy rain.

04:42.948 --> 04:46.744
Wind over forty kilometres an hour: tie down loose things.

04:47.714 --> 04:52.143
Before you leave for the job: the FHA is read and signed by all crew.

04:52.093 --> 04:54.361
Feet measured, parts counted.

04:54.361 --> 04:56.051
Plan sheet done.

04:56.051 --> 04:59.454
Box spot picked, and GFCI plug found.

04:59.454 --> 05:00.947
Parts loaded.

05:00.947 --> 05:03.458
Clamp meter and multimeter in the truck.

05:03.458 --> 05:05.553
Tools loaded and charged.

05:05.553 --> 05:08.250
Client time confirmed, weather checked.

05:08.250 --> 05:10.912
Ladder checked, and PPE packed.

05:11.866 --> 05:13.100
Recap.

05:13.050 --> 05:15.735
FHA signed before work starts.

05:15.735 --> 05:18.860
Ladder at four to one, with a partner at the base.

05:18.860 --> 05:22.066
Three points of contact, one limb at a time.

05:22.066 --> 05:25.678
GFCI plug, and unplug before box work.

05:25.678 --> 05:29.752
Glasses, gloves, boots, hi-vis and hard hat.

05:29.752 --> 05:32.529
And be on time, tidy and respectful.

05:33.498 --> 05:35.160
That's Part 7.

05:35.110 --> 05:38.131
Now take the Part 7 quiz in your training portal.
